# MEASURED, not assumed: Cloudflare Pages CONCATENATES Cache-Control from every
# rule whose path matches. It does NOT let a more specific path override a broader
# one, and file order does not change that. A first attempt declared
#
#   /*               Cache-Control: no-cache, must-revalidate
#   /_next/static/*  Cache-Control: public, max-age=31536000, immutable
#
# and the edge served the two joined together:
#
#   cache-control: no-cache, must-revalidate, public, max-age=31536000, immutable
#   cf-cache-status: BYPASS
#
# The leading no-cache wins on any conforming cache, so the year-long immutable was
# dead on arrival and hashed chunks stayed uncached. There is no negative-match or
# exclusion syntax in _headers to carve paths out of /*.
#
# So /* deliberately declares NO Cache-Control. HTML falls through to the Pages
# default (public, max-age=0, must-revalidate — revalidate on every request), which
# is the policy we wanted for HTML anyway. Only the two cacheable asset classes name
# the header, so nothing concatenates onto them.
#
# Do not add Cache-Control to /*. It will silently poison every rule below.

# Build output is content-hashed (e.g. /_next/static/chunks/0d4d8j3tgmov7.js). The
# filename changes whenever the bytes change, so a stale copy is unreachable by
# definition and the response can be frozen for a year.
/_next/static/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=31536000, immutable

# Gateway wall tiles are stable but NOT content-hashed (wall-0.webp … wall-11.webp),
# so they are rewritten in place by the build. One hour of edge caching plus a forced
# revalidation after that bounds how long a replaced tile can be served stale.
/gateway-wall/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=3600, must-revalidate

# THE SERVICE WORKER. This is the single most safety-critical caching decision on the site,
# so it is declared rather than inherited.
#
# It already resolved correctly by accident: with no rule, /sw.js fell through to the Pages
# default (public, max-age=0, must-revalidate), which is exactly right. Stating it means a
# future change to the default cannot silently extend how long a broken worker — or a
# superseded one — survives in the wild.
#
# The kill switch depends on this line. scripts/sw-kill.js works by being deployed OVER
# public/sw.js; the browser byte-compares the script on every navigation, so a worker that
# is never edge-cached is a worker that can be replaced within one navigation for every
# reader. Cache this file and the kill switch becomes as slow as whatever TTL you set.
#
# Safe to declare despite the concatenation hazard documented at the top of this file:
# /* declares no Cache-Control, so nothing joins onto this rule.
/sw.js
  Cache-Control: no-cache, must-revalidate
  Service-Worker-Allowed: /

# The manifest is emitted by app/manifest.js (Next's file convention) as
# /manifest.webmanifest. Cloudflare Pages generally types .webmanifest correctly; saying it
# outright removes a silent-failure mode, since a manifest served as the wrong type is
# ignored by the installability check with no error anywhere a developer would look — and
# on iOS an uninstallable site is a shelf that Safari evicts after seven days.
/manifest.webmanifest
  Content-Type: application/manifest+json; charset=utf-8
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=3600, must-revalidate
